<main id="main" class="page">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-title ds-text-gradient ds-text-gradient--animated">How it works</h1>
    <p class="page-sub">Everything runs in your browser. Nothing about your machine is uploaded.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="how-section">
    <h2>Detection</h2>
    <p>
      We read the WebGL renderer string, <code>navigator.deviceMemory</code>,
      <code>navigator.hardwareConcurrency</code>, and the user agent. VRAM comes from a GPU database, unified memory on
      Apple Silicon, or an estimate from RAM. Chromium caps <code>deviceMemory</code> at 8 GB, so the chip is
      cross-checked on Apple machines.
    </p>

    <h2>Ranking</h2>
    <p>
      Models are filtered to those that fit in your VRAM at Q4, then ranked by capability against the resource cost. The
      trade-off slider leans the ranking toward speed or toward capability. Tight fits are marked because a model that
      barely fits has less room for context.
    </p>

    <h2>Speed</h2>
    <p>
      Speed is an estimate, not a benchmark. Decode is bandwidth-bound, so the estimate uses your GPU or chip memory
      bandwidth, a backend efficiency coefficient, and the active weights of the model. Sparse models only read their
      active parameters per token. The result is shown as a band, and the terminal is a simulation at that speed.
    </p>

    <h2>Cost</h2>
    <p>
      Local is a fixed cost plus electricity. Cloud is a marginal price per token. The calculator shows a frontier model
      and DeepSeek side by side because the gap between public prices is the useful part. Cloud prices come from
      models.dev, with four input tokens per output token.
    </p>

    <h2>Data</h2>
    <p>
      Model data comes from models.dev and the Auxot model registry, refreshed by a weekly job. GPU bandwidth comes from
      GPU Ark. Apple bandwidth is curated from Apple's spec pages. The <a href="/credits/">credits</a> page lists the
      sources.
    </p>
  </section>

